WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:07.336
Kubernetes already restarts a container whenever its process exits.

2
00:00:07.567 --> 00:00:10.389
But an app can stay up and still be useless.

3
00:00:10.389 --> 00:00:13.660
Stuck, or running but unable to reach its database.

4
00:00:13.867 --> 00:00:18.991
A probe is a small check that the kubelet runs on a timer.

5
00:00:18.991 --> 00:00:20.581
Here, an HTTP GET.

6
00:00:20.581 --> 00:00:24.468
Any status from 200 to 399 counts as a pass.

7
00:00:25.300 --> 00:00:26.684
There are three probes.

8
00:00:26.684 --> 00:00:31.137
Each asks a different question, and a failure leads to a different action.

9
00:00:31.367 --> 00:00:34.576
The startup probe asks: have you finished starting?

10
00:00:34.576 --> 00:00:36.841
Until it passes, the other two wait.

11
00:00:37.067 --> 00:00:40.734
In the lab, it allows thirty failures, two seconds apart.

12
00:00:40.734 --> 00:00:43.565
So the app gets up to sixty seconds to boot.

13
00:00:43.767 --> 00:00:48.979
The moment the app answers, startup passes, and hands over to liveness and readiness.

14
00:00:49.200 --> 00:00:51.823
The liveness probe asks: are you stuck?

15
00:00:51.823 --> 00:00:54.312
It checks /healthz every ten seconds.

16
00:00:54.533 --> 00:00:59.540
After three failures in a row, the kubelet kills the container and starts a new one.

17
00:00:59.540 --> 00:01:02.163
In the lab, that takes about thirty seconds.

18
00:01:02.367 --> 00:01:06.271
The readiness probe asks: can you take traffic right now?

19
00:01:06.271 --> 00:01:08.806
It checks /readyz every five seconds.

20
00:01:08.806 --> 00:01:10.792
And /readyz also pings Redis.

21
00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:16.824
After two misses, the Pod gets no traffic from Services, within about ten seconds.

22
00:01:16.824 --> 00:01:18.032
Nothing restarts.

23
00:01:18.833 --> 00:01:19.692
Now the trap.

24
00:01:19.692 --> 00:01:25.439
Never make the liveness probe depend on something outside the container, such as Redis.

25
00:01:25.667 --> 00:01:28.179
Picture Redis going down for a minute.

26
00:01:28.179 --> 00:01:33.866
Every Pod fails liveness at about the same time, and the kubelet restarts all of them.

27
00:01:33.866 --> 00:01:35.585
Restarts cannot fix Redis.

28
00:01:35.800 --> 00:01:41.206
Dependency checks belong in readiness, because failing it only pauses traffic.

29
00:01:41.206 --> 00:01:42.384
Nothing restarts.

30
00:01:43.200 --> 00:01:48.090
Each container can say how much CPU and memory it needs.

31
00:01:48.090 --> 00:01:52.195
100m means 100 millicores, a tenth of one core.

32
00:01:52.195 --> 00:01:54.465
128Mi means 128 mebibytes.

33
00:01:54.465 --> 00:01:58.395
There are two numbers, a request and a limit.

34
00:01:58.600 --> 00:02:01.365
A request is what the scheduler reserves.

35
00:02:01.365 --> 00:02:05.952
It puts a Pod only on a node whose unreserved room fits the request.

36
00:02:06.167 --> 00:02:08.937
This new Pod does not fit on the first node.

37
00:02:08.937 --> 00:02:10.575
It does fit on the second.

38
00:02:10.800 --> 00:02:14.620
If no node has room for the request, the Pod stays Pending.

39
00:02:14.620 --> 00:02:17.469
The scheduler counts requests, not live use.

40
00:02:18.300 --> 00:02:20.961
A limit is the most the container may use.

41
00:02:20.961 --> 00:02:25.268
The Linux kernel enforces it while the container runs, with cgroups.

42
00:02:25.500 --> 00:02:29.063
At the limit, the two resources behave very differently.

43
00:02:29.063 --> 00:02:30.844
Too much CPU makes you slow.

44
00:02:30.844 --> 00:02:32.880
Too much memory gets you killed.

45
00:02:33.100 --> 00:02:37.204
The kernel enforces a CPU limit as a time budget.

46
00:02:37.204 --> 00:02:43.737
With 200m, the container gets 20 milliseconds of CPU in every 100, then waits.

47
00:02:43.967 --> 00:02:46.660
It waits even when other cores sit idle.

48
00:02:46.660 --> 00:02:47.940
That is throttling.

49
00:02:47.940 --> 00:02:49.960
It keeps running, only slower.

50
00:02:50.167 --> 00:02:53.413
Memory cannot be taken back once a process holds it.

51
00:02:53.413 --> 00:02:58.095
Between request and limit, a container may use whatever the node has spare.

52
00:02:58.300 --> 00:03:03.584
Over the memory limit, the kernel's out of memory killer stops it.

53
00:03:03.584 --> 00:03:08.468
Kubernetes shows OOMKilled, exit code 137: signal 9, SIGKILL.

54
00:03:09.300 --> 00:03:12.024
The lab sets no CPU limit, on purpose.

55
00:03:12.024 --> 00:03:15.536
For a web app like this one, rely on the request.

56
00:03:15.536 --> 00:03:17.256
But always set requests.

57
00:03:18.067 --> 00:03:23.606
A HorizontalPodAutoscaler changes the number of replicas of a Deployment for you.

58
00:03:23.606 --> 00:03:27.915
It is one more control loop, and it runs every fifteen seconds.

59
00:03:28.133 --> 00:03:33.559
It reads each Pod's CPU use, as a percentage of the Pod's CPU request.

60
00:03:33.767 --> 00:03:37.906
The lab targets 70 percent of the 100m request.

61
00:03:37.906 --> 00:03:39.668
That is 70m per Pod.

62
00:03:39.900 --> 00:03:43.119
Say three Pods average 140m under load.

63
00:03:43.119 --> 00:03:45.183
That is twice the target.

64
00:03:45.400 --> 00:03:51.206
So it works out how many replicas would bring the average back to the target.

65
00:03:51.206 --> 00:03:53.694
Ceil of three times 140, over 70.

66
00:03:53.694 --> 00:03:53.996
Six.

67
00:03:54.200 --> 00:04:00.585
Then it sets the Deployment's replica count, between minReplicas, three, and maxReplicas, six.

68
00:04:00.800 --> 00:04:04.070
It scales up at once, but down slowly, on purpose.

69
00:04:04.070 --> 00:04:08.844
By default it wants five minutes of lower numbers before it removes Pods.

70
00:04:08.844 --> 00:04:10.740
The lab shortens that to two.

71
00:04:11.367 --> 00:04:13.881
It needs metrics-server for the numbers.

72
00:04:13.881 --> 00:04:18.847
And without a CPU request, it has no percentage to compute, so it does nothing.

73
00:04:19.667 --> 00:04:23.504
Probes decide when to wait, restart, or stop traffic.

74
00:04:23.504 --> 00:04:25.893
Requests decide where a Pod fits.

75
00:04:25.893 --> 00:04:29.658
At the limit, CPU is throttled and memory is killed.

76
00:04:29.658 --> 00:04:32.771
And the HPA adds Pods from the average CPU.
